WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:05.235
P6.1 · Valuation cases, methods and drivers

2
00:00:06.385 --> 00:00:13.528
A valuation is a case: a date, a currency and its inputs. Keep as many as you need on the same company.

3
00:00:13.578 --> 00:00:18.546
Duplicate copies a case, so you change one assumption and keep the other intact.

4
00:00:19.696 --> 00:00:24.562
Each case opens on its value: WACC, enterprise value, equity value.

5
00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:28.143
Duplicate makes the next case; All cases lists them.

6
00:00:29.293 --> 00:00:34.965
The DCF discounts the projected free cash flows at the WACC, plus a terminal value.

7
00:00:35.015 --> 00:00:41.892
Comparables apply the peers' multiples. By default each family weighs half; change the weights if you need to.

8
00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:49.879
With a budget, the projection is the budget, company by company. Later years are extrapolated, and labelled.

9
00:00:49.929 --> 00:00:54.897
Without a budget, the case projects from twelve months of the ledger and your drivers.

10
00:00:56.047 --> 00:01:04.593
Revenue and margin come from the books. Risk premiums, betas and multiples come from public datasets by country and industry.

11
00:01:04.643 --> 00:01:08.039
You enter the risk-free rate and any specific premium.

12
00:01:09.189 --> 00:01:14.338
Every input sits in the panel on the right, with its source: dataset, ledger, or you.

13
00:01:14.388 --> 00:01:17.811
Change one, click Recompute, and the value follows.

14
00:01:19.189 --> 00:01:26.164
The sensitivity grid shows what moves the value most: one point of WACC against one point of terminal growth.

15
00:01:26.214 --> 00:01:30.954
Warnings flag the traps, like a terminal value carrying most of the value.

16
00:01:32.404 --> 00:01:36.159
Next: P6.2  Value over time: what moved it

